{"name":"Guava Jelly","hex":"#ee9685","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"guava","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tropical tree or shrub of the myrtle family, Psidium guajava.","Its yellowish tropical fruit, globular or pear-shaped with thin, yellow, green or brown skin, is often made into jams and jellies."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/guava"},{"term":"jelly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dessert made by boiling gelatine (or a plant-based alternative such as agar or carrageenan), sugar and some flavouring (often derived from fruit) and allowing it to set.","A clear or translucent fruit preserve, made from fruit juice and set using either naturally occurring, or added, pectin."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make into jelly.","To preserve in jelly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jelly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Goiabada","blocks":[{"text":"Goiabada is a conserve made of red guavas and sugar, commonly found throughout the Portuguese-speaking countries of the world. It dates back to the colonial times of Brazil, where guavas were used as a substitute for the quinces used to make quince cheese. It required an abundance of sugar and slave labor for its production as it was made in large batches within cauldrons cooking over a slow fire. In rural areas of Brazil, it is still commonly made at home for family use or sale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Goiabada_slice.jpg/330px-Goiabada_slice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goiabada","wikidata":"Q1459788"},{"title":"Bocadillo (dessert)","blocks":[{"text":"Bocadillo, guava jelly, or guava paste, is a Hispanic American confection made with guava pulp and panela, which is consumed abundantly throughout Colombia, Costa Rica, Ecuador, Panama and Venezuela. Similar confection can be produced from other fruits like banana and coconut."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Bocadillo.jpg/330px-Bocadillo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bocadillo_(dessert)","wikidata":"Q529941"},{"title":"Guava Jelly (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Guava Jelly\" is a song recorded by the Jamaican group Bob Marley and the Wailers. It was released as a 7\" vinyl single through Tuff Gong and Green Door Records. It was issued commercially with B-side track \"Redder Then Red\", which was misspelled on its initial printing, in 1971. It was written and produced by Marley and features uncredited lyrical contributions from Bunny Livingston. A reggae composition like the majority of Marley's works, \"Guava Jelly\" contains a rocksteady and island-like production with lyrics loosely based around sexual intercourse. His use of the term \"guava jelly\" was likely referring to a specific type of sexual lubricant. It was favorably viewed by several reviewers, with many of them finding the composition to be sexual and about love. The group placed \"Guava Jelly\" on several compilation albums, including Africa Unite: The Singles Collection in 2005, and Owen Gray and Herbie Mann created their own versions in 1974 and 1975, respectively."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/58/Bob_Marley_%22Guava_Jelly%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guava_Jelly_(song)","wikidata":"Q28453385"},{"title":"Guava","blocks":[{"text":"Guava, also known as the 'guava-pear' in various regions, is a common tropical fruit cultivated in many tropical and subtropical regions. The common guava Psidium guajava is a small tree in the myrtle family (Myrtaceae), native to Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean and northern South America. Botanically, guavas are berries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Guava_pink_fruit.jpg/330px-Guava_pink_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guava","wikidata":"Q3181909"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_-02.jpg/500px-Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_-02.jpg/500px-Feijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFeijoada_%C3%A0_brasileira_-02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Gleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg/500px-Gleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Gleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg/1280px-Gleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Gleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg/500px-Gleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Marley's mention of the term \"guava jelly\" may be referring to the use of lubricant products during sexual intercourse.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGleitmittel_Personal_Lubricants.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8b/Johnny_Nash_%22Guava_Jelly%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8b/Johnny_Nash_%22Guava_Jelly%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohnny_Nash_%22Guava_Jelly%22.jpg"},{"